Director of Capability, Armament and Planning (CAP)
EDA - European Defence Agency
Brussels, Belgium
€ 18.695,13 per month gross
DUTIES
The Capability, Armament and Planning (CAP) Director is accountable to the Chief Executive as Head of the CAP Directorate.
Supported by the Heads of Unit and Office, the jobholder will be responsible for providing strategic leadership and direction to the Agency’s activities in capability development, defence planning, strategic analysis and armaments cooperation, supporting Member States in collaboratively planning, developing and acquiring defence capabilities that respond to their needs and to agreed EU defence priorities.
The CAP Directorate leads EDA’s work on the Capability Development Plan (CDP) and the Coordinated Annual Review on Defence (CARD), supports capability development across the Air and Space, Land and Maritime domains and relevant strategic enabling and support areas. Together with Member States and with the contribution of relevant EU institutions and bodies, the Directorate operates the EU Capability Development Planning System (EU-CDPS) and supports the implementation of agreed capability priorities through collaborative activities, projects and programmes.
Reporting to the Chief Executive, the CAP Director will have overall managerial responsibility for the Directorate and provide strategic advice on capability development, defence planning, strategic analysis and armaments cooperation. The jobholder will drive cooperation with Member States and engage with relevant EU institutions, bodies and international partners, ensuring that agreed priorities are translated into coherent and effective capability outcomes.
More specifically, the jobholder will be responsible for the following activities:
translate the Agency’s mission, strategy, work programme and priorities into a coherent vision, objectives and deliverables for the Directorate;
provide strategic vision, direction and leadership for the Directorate, ensuring coherence across its capability development, defence planning, strategic analysis and armaments cooperation activities;
lead and oversee capability development activities across the Air and Space, Land and Maritime domains, as well as in the areas of resilience and support, strategic enablers and cyber, ensuring that they address Member States’ current and future capability needs and contribute to agreed EU defence priorities;
steer the development and implementation of the Capability Development Plan (CDP), including the identification and prioritisation of common capability needs and priorities at EU level;
lead EDA’s work on the Coordinated Annual Review on Defence (CARD), supporting the analysis of Member States’ defence plans and the identification of recommendations and opportunities for European cooperation;
provide strategic direction and oversee EDA’s contribution to Permanent Structured Cooperation (PESCO), in close cooperation with the European External Action Service (EEAS), including the EU Military Staff;
steer the implementation of agreed capability priorities through collaborative roadmaps and preparatory activities, including the harmonisation of capability requirements, the development of business cases and the identification and development of collaborative capability and armaments projects and programmes, as well as opportunities for aggregation of demand and joint procurement in close cooperation with the Procurement, Support, Industry and Interoperability Directorate (PSII);
provide strategic leadership for the Directorate’s contribution to the Annual Defence Readiness Report, ensuring together with the EDA Policy and Management Office (EPMO) robust capability analysis and actionable recommendations, and drive support for the development and implementation of initiatives within the Priority Capability Areas (PCAs), including EDPCIs, in close cooperation with Member States and relevant EU institutions and bodies;
promote coherence and synergies between national, EU and multinational defence planning and capability development activities, including relevant links with PESCO, the European Defence Fund (EDF), the EU Defence Readiness objective and other EU initiatives;
build and sustain strong cooperation with Member States, the European Commission, the Council, the EEAS, OCCAR, NATO and other relevant European and international organisations and partners in the fields covered by the Directorate;
ensure the Directorate’s effective contribution to EDA’s transversal activities and close cooperation with EDA’s other Directorates;
lead, motivate and develop the competences and skills of the Directorate’s staff and evaluate or contribute to the evaluation of their performance;
plan and organise the work and the financial and human resources of the Directorate, ensuring the effective and efficient use of allocated resources;
manage the Directorate’s budget through sound planning and monitoring of allocated resources;
take on additional tasks as required in the interest of the service.
Duties may evolve depending on the development of the EDA’s structure and activities and decisions of EDA management.
ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA
To be considered eligible to take part in this selection procedure, on the closing date for the submission of applications candidates must satisfy all the eligibility criteria as specified below:
be a national of a Member State participating in the Agency;
be entitled to their full rights as citizens;
have fulfilled any obligations imposed on them by the laws concerning military service;
produce the appropriate character references as to their suitability for the performance of their duties (extract from the “judicial record” or certificate of good conduct will be requested prior to recruitment);
be physically fit to perform their duties;
have a thorough knowledge (minimum level C1 oral and written) of one of the languages of the participating Member States and a satisfactory knowledge (minimum level B2 oral and written) of another of these languages to the extent necessary to discharge their duties;
have no personal interest (financial, family relationship, or other) which could be in conflict with disinterested discharge of their duties within the Agency;
hold, or be in a position to obtain, a valid Personnel Security Clearance Certificate (national or EU PSC at SECRET UE/EU SECRET level). Personnel Security Clearance Certificate (PSCC) means a certificate issued by a competent authority establishing that an individual is security cleared and holds a valid national or EU PSC, and which shows the level of EUCI to which that individual may be granted access (SECRET UE/EU SECRET), the date of validity of the relevant PSC and the date of expiry of the certificate itself. Note that the necessary procedure for obtaining a PSCC can be initiated on request of the employer only, and not by the individual candidate;
have a level of education which corresponds to completed university studies attested by a diploma when the normal period of university education is four years or more, or a level of education which corresponds to completed university studies attested by a diploma and appropriate professional experience of at least one year when the normal period of university education is at least three years or be a graduate of a national or international Defence College; or where justified in the interests of the service, professional training of an equivalent level.
Only diplomas that have been awarded in EU Member States or that are the subject of equivalence certificates issued by the authorities in the said Member States shall be taken into consideration. In the latter case, the authority authorised to conclude contracts of employment reserves the right to request proof of such equivalence.
For diplomas awarded in non-EU countries, a NARIC recognition is required.
Qualifications/diplomas awarded until 31/12/2020 in the United Kingdom are accepted without further recognition. For diplomas awarded after this date (from 01/01/2021), a NARIC recognition is required.
For native English speakers, your ability to communicate in another EU language will be tested during the selection process.
SELECTION CRITERIA
